# Change Brief — <change-id>

Write this artifact in the configured report language. Keep code tokens, identifiers, paths, commands, table names, and conventional technical terms exact.

## Current situation

Describe how the system behaves now and only the facts needed to understand this change.

## Problem to solve

Describe the concrete user-visible or runtime problem. Start with observable behavior, not architecture vocabulary.

## Planned change

Explain what will change in plain language. Describe the behavior first. Introduce a technical term only when it helps the developer read the code later, and define it on first use.

## Expected impact

List the main files, boundaries, interfaces, or runtime behavior likely to change. Explain each item in one short sentence.

## Risks and checks

State what could go wrong and how the change will be checked. Include alternatives only when the choice materially affects understanding; do not add an alternatives section by habit.

<!-- Readability rule: concrete behavior -> reason -> technical term. Prefer wording a developer would naturally use when explaining the change to a teammate. Avoid literal translation-like nouns and unnecessary abstraction. -->
